(function (g) {
  g.MODELS = (g.MODELS || []).concat([
    { id: 't2-p43', task: 2, ref: 'p43', band: '8.0', words: 283,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Rising rates of anxiety and depression|Tỉ lệ lo âu và trầm cảm ngày càng tăng]] among teenagers have led to a debate about who should be responsible for young people's mental health. {{While}} some argue that it deserves [[a dedicated place in the timetable|một vị trí riêng trong thời khóa biểu]], others believe it should be left to families and doctors. In my view, schools should teach it, but [[in close cooperation with|phối hợp chặt chẽ với]] parents and professionals.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Those who oppose a separate subject [[have a valid point|có lý lẽ xác đáng]]. <<Giải thích>> Mental health is [[a deeply personal matter|một vấn đề hết sức riêng tư]], and parents usually understand their own child's character and difficulties far better than a teacher responsible for thirty pupils. {{Furthermore}}, serious conditions require [[trained psychologists|các nhà tâm lý học được đào tạo bài bản]] rather than classroom lessons, and [[poorly designed courses|những khóa học được thiết kế kém]] might even [[trivialise genuine suffering|xem nhẹ những nỗi đau thực sự]].

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, a school subject can [[reach every child|tiếp cận mọi trẻ em]], including those whose families are unable or unwilling to talk about emotions. <<Giải thích>> Regular lessons can teach pupils to [[recognise the warning signs|nhận biết các dấu hiệu cảnh báo]] of stress, [[cope with setbacks|đối phó với thất bại]] and ask for help without feeling ashamed. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, schools in Australia that introduced such programmes have reported fewer cases of bullying and [[a greater willingness to seek support|sự sẵn lòng tìm kiếm hỗ trợ cao hơn]]. Such lessons also give pupils [[a shared vocabulary|vốn từ chung]] for discussing feelings with friends and teachers.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> I believe [[the two approaches are complementary|hai cách tiếp cận bổ sung cho nhau]] rather than competing. Schools are best placed to provide [[basic knowledge and early detection|kiến thức nền tảng và phát hiện sớm]], {{whereas}} families offer daily emotional support and professionals treat more serious problems. A dedicated subject would also help to [[break the stigma|xóa bỏ sự kỳ thị]] that still surrounds mental illness in many communities.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} families and health experts [[play an indispensable role|đóng vai trò không thể thay thế]], mental health should be taught as a separate subject, provided that teachers are properly trained and work hand in hand with parents and specialists.` },

    { id: 't2-p44', task: 2, ref: 'p44', band: '8.0', words: 280,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> As urban populations continue to grow, planners face [[a fundamental choice|một lựa chọn mang tính căn bản]]: should cities spread outwards into the surrounding countryside or [[build upwards|phát triển theo chiều cao]] on land that is already in use? {{Although}} both options have merits, I believe vertical growth is [[the more sustainable path|con đường bền vững hơn]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Supporters of suburban expansion argue that it offers families [[a higher quality of life|chất lượng cuộc sống cao hơn]]. <<Giải thích>> New suburbs typically provide larger homes, private gardens and quieter streets at [[a fraction of the price|một phần nhỏ của mức giá]] of a city-centre flat. {{In addition}}, building on [[open land|đất trống]] is often cheaper and faster than [[redeveloping crowded districts|tái phát triển các khu đông đúc]], which helps to ease housing shortages quickly. Many young families also value [[the sense of safety|cảm giác an toàn]] that low-density neighbourhoods provide for children playing outdoors.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, others contend that building upwards makes far better use of [[scarce land|quỹ đất khan hiếm]]. <<Giải thích>> When people live closer together, public transport, schools and hospitals can serve them more efficiently, and residents spend less time [[stuck in traffic|kẹt xe]]. Urban sprawl, {{by contrast}}, [[swallows up farmland and forests|nuốt chửng đất nông nghiệp và rừng]] and makes car ownership almost essential. <<Ví dụ>> Singapore, {{for instance}}, houses millions of people in [[high-rise estates|các khu nhà cao tầng]] while [[setting aside|dành riêng]] a large share of its territory for parks and nature reserves.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> In my opinion, the environmental and economic costs of sprawl are [[too high to ignore|quá lớn để có thể bỏ qua]]. Long commutes increase [[carbon emissions|lượng khí thải carbon]], and stretching roads and water pipes over ever larger areas [[places a heavy burden on|đặt gánh nặng lớn lên]] public budgets. Well-designed tower blocks with green spaces and [[community facilities|các tiện ích cộng đồng]] can provide comfortable homes without destroying the countryside.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} new suburbs may offer more space and [[short-term relief|giải pháp tạm thời]] for housing shortages, I am convinced that cities should prioritise building upwards, as this protects natural land and creates [[more efficient communities|những cộng đồng vận hành hiệu quả hơn]].` },

    { id: 't2-p46', task: 2, ref: 'p46', band: '8.0', words: 284,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Mass tourism|Du lịch đại trà]] has placed enormous pressure on famous cities and natural wonders, prompting some authorities to [[charge visitors an entry fee|thu phí vào cửa đối với du khách]]. {{While}} critics claim this is unfair to poorer travellers, I believe such fees are justified if they are [[carefully designed|được thiết kế cẩn thận]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Those who support entry fees point to [[the damage caused by overcrowding|thiệt hại do tình trạng quá tải gây ra]]. <<Giải thích>> Huge numbers of visitors [[wear down|làm xuống cấp]] ancient buildings, pollute beaches and push up rents for local residents. A fee can [[discourage casual day-trippers|hạn chế những du khách ghé qua trong ngày]] and generate money for maintenance and conservation. <<Ví dụ>> Venice, {{for example}}, introduced a charge for day visitors in order to [[ease congestion|giảm ùn tắc]] in its narrow streets and fund the protection of its historic centre.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, opponents argue that fees turn travel into [[a privilege of the wealthy|đặc quyền của người giàu]]. <<Giải thích>> For families on modest incomes, even a small charge [[adds up|cộng dồn lại thành khoản lớn]] when combined with transport and accommodation, and they may be [[priced out of|bị loại khỏi vì giá cao]] experiences that should belong to everyone. {{Moreover}}, wealthy tourists, who often cause just as much disruption, would [[hardly notice|hầu như không nhận thấy]] the extra cost. Such a policy could also [[deepen social divisions|làm sâu sắc thêm sự chia rẽ xã hội]] by reserving cultural treasures for those who can afford them.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> In my view, the solution lies in [[a flexible pricing system|một hệ thống giá linh hoạt]] rather than abolishing fees altogether. Charges could be higher during [[peak seasons|mùa cao điểm]] and lower or free for students, children and low-income groups. {{Crucially}}, the revenue should be [[reinvested in|tái đầu tư vào]] the site and the local community, so that visitors can see the benefit of what they pay.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} entry fees may [[place a burden on|tạo gánh nặng cho]] less affluent travellers, I believe they are necessary to protect popular destinations, provided that discounts and free days ensure that everyone still has [[a fair chance to visit|cơ hội công bằng để tham quan]].` },

    { id: 't2-p49', task: 2, ref: 'p49', band: '8.0', words: 281,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In many developed and developing countries alike, couples are [[having fewer children|sinh ít con hơn]] or choosing not to become parents at all. This [[demographic shift|sự thay đổi nhân khẩu học]] creates serious economic and social challenges, but a combination of family-friendly policies and [[workforce reforms|cải cách lực lượng lao động]] could help to address them.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> [[The most pressing problem|Vấn đề cấp bách nhất]] is a shrinking workforce supporting [[an ageing population|dân số già hóa]]. <<Giải thích>> As fewer young people enter the job market, there are not enough taxpayers to fund pensions and healthcare for the growing number of retirees. Businesses also [[struggle to fill vacancies|gặp khó khăn trong việc tuyển đủ người]], which can [[slow economic growth|làm chậm tăng trưởng kinh tế]]. <<Ví dụ>> Japan and South Korea, {{for example}}, already face labour shortages in sectors such as nursing and construction. {{In addition}}, rural schools and villages may [[empty out|trở nên vắng bóng người]] as the population declines.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Governments can [[tackle the root causes|giải quyết tận gốc nguyên nhân]] by making parenthood more affordable. <<Giải thích>> Many couples delay having children because of [[soaring housing costs|giá nhà tăng vọt]] and expensive childcare. Generous parental leave, subsidised nurseries and [[tax incentives|ưu đãi thuế]] for families would ease this burden, while flexible working arrangements would help parents [[balance careers and family life|cân bằng sự nghiệp và cuộc sống gia đình]]. Nordic countries have shown that such support can [[stabilise birth rates|ổn định tỉ lệ sinh]] to some extent.

<<Thân bài 3>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{At the same time}}, societies must adapt to [[the new reality|thực tế mới]]. Raising the retirement age gradually, encouraging older people to keep working [[on a part-time basis|theo hình thức bán thời gian]] and welcoming skilled immigrants could all help to [[offset labour shortages|bù đắp tình trạng thiếu hụt lao động]]. Investment in automation would also allow industries to remain productive with fewer workers. Robots already [[assist carers|hỗ trợ người chăm sóc]] in some Japanese nursing homes, {{for instance}}.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, falling birth rates threaten [[public finances|tài chính công]] and economic growth. Governments should both support families who wish to have children and adapt their labour markets so that society can [[remain prosperous|tiếp tục thịnh vượng]] with a smaller population.` },

    { id: 't2-p50', task: 2, ref: 'p50', band: '8.0', words: 287,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[A staggering amount of|Một lượng lớn đáng kinh ngạc]] edible food ends up in rubbish bins every day, even though millions of people around the world [[go hungry|bị đói]]. This waste [[stems from|bắt nguồn từ]] the habits of consumers and businesses alike, but it can be reduced through education, regulation and better use of technology.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> At household level, food is wasted mainly because of [[poor planning|việc lên kế hoạch kém]] and confusion over labels. <<Giải thích>> Many people [[buy in bulk|mua số lượng lớn]] to take advantage of special offers and then forget what is in the fridge. Others throw away perfectly safe products as soon as they pass their [["best before" date|hạn "sử dụng tốt nhất trước"]], [[mistaking it for|nhầm nó với]] a safety deadline. <<Giải thích>> Supermarkets and restaurants, {{meanwhile}}, [[reject imperfect-looking produce|loại bỏ nông sản có hình thức không hoàn hảo]], overstock shelves to appear generous and serve portions that are [[far larger than|lớn hơn nhiều so với]] customers can eat.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Several measures could [[make a real difference|tạo ra sự khác biệt thực sự]]. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, governments could require large supermarkets to [[donate unsold food|quyên góp thực phẩm chưa bán được]] to charities rather than discarding it. <<Ví dụ>> France introduced such a law in 2016, and food banks now receive millions of extra meals each year. {{Secondly}}, clearer labelling and public campaigns would help consumers understand that many products remain safe [[well beyond|lâu hơn nhiều so với]] the printed date. {{Finally}}, mobile apps that allow cafés and bakeries to sell leftover items [[at a discount|với giá giảm]] at the end of the day have proved both popular and effective.

<<Thân bài 3>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Individuals [[also have a part to play|cũng có vai trò của mình]]. Writing shopping lists, storing food correctly and [[making creative use of leftovers|tận dụng thức ăn thừa một cách sáng tạo]] are simple habits that can save both money and resources. Even freezing bread or fruit before it [[goes off|bị hỏng]] can prevent a surprising amount of waste.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, food waste is caused by careless shopping, [[misleading labels|nhãn mác gây hiểu lầm]] and commercial practices. A combination of legislation, technology and [[personal responsibility|trách nhiệm cá nhân]] is needed to tackle the problem effectively.` },

    { id: 't2-p51', task: 2, ref: 'p51', band: '8.0', words: 284,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Fraudulent schemes|Các thủ đoạn lừa đảo]] aimed at elderly people have become alarmingly widespread, with victims losing [[their life savings|tiền tiết kiệm cả đời]] to fake phone calls, emails and websites. This trend has several underlying causes, but families, banks and governments can all [[take steps to protect|thực hiện các biện pháp để bảo vệ]] vulnerable older citizens.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reason is that many older people are [[less familiar with digital technology|ít quen thuộc với công nghệ số]] than younger generations. <<Giải thích>> As banking, shopping and public services move online, they are [[forced to adapt|buộc phải thích nghi]] to systems they do not fully understand, which makes it difficult to [[distinguish genuine messages from fake ones|phân biệt tin nhắn thật với tin nhắn giả]]. {{Moreover}}, criminals deliberately [[exploit loneliness and trust|lợi dụng sự cô đơn và lòng tin]], posing as relatives in trouble or as bank officials. <<Giải thích>> Older people also tend to have [[substantial savings|khoản tiết kiệm đáng kể]], making them attractive targets, and many feel too embarrassed to report a scam, so offenders are [[rarely caught|hiếm khi bị bắt]].

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> [[Tackling this problem|Giải quyết vấn đề này]] requires action on several fronts. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, banks should introduce stronger safeguards, such as delaying unusually large transfers and calling customers to [[confirm suspicious transactions|xác nhận các giao dịch đáng ngờ]]. {{Secondly}}, community centres and libraries could offer free [[digital literacy classes|các lớp kỹ năng số]] that teach older people how common scams work. <<Ví dụ>> In the UK, {{for instance}}, banks have already prevented many losses by training staff to [[spot the warning signs|nhận ra các dấu hiệu cảnh báo]] when an elderly customer withdraws an unusual sum.

<<Thân bài 3>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Families [[also have a vital role|cũng có vai trò thiết yếu]]. Younger relatives should talk openly with their parents and grandparents about online risks and encourage them to [[double-check|kiểm tra lại]] any request for money. Governments, {{meanwhile}}, should [[impose tougher penalties|áp dụng hình phạt nghiêm khắc hơn]] on fraudsters and [[cooperate across borders|hợp tác xuyên biên giới]], since many scams are run from abroad.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, online scams targeting older people are increasing because of limited digital skills and criminals' [[ruthless tactics|thủ đoạn tàn nhẫn]]. Better banking safeguards, education and family support are essential to [[keep them safe|giữ an toàn cho họ]].` },

    { id: 't2-p52', task: 2, ref: 'p52', band: '8.0', words: 277,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Navigation apps have become [[an indispensable tool|một công cụ không thể thiếu]] for drivers, cyclists and pedestrians, and few people now bother to memorise routes or [[consult a paper map|xem bản đồ giấy]]. {{Although}} this dependence has some drawbacks, I believe it is [[on balance|xét tổng thể]] a positive development.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The most obvious advantage is [[efficiency|tính hiệu quả]]. <<Giải thích>> Navigation apps calculate the fastest route [[in real time|theo thời gian thực]], warn users about traffic jams and accidents, and suggest alternatives [[within seconds|chỉ trong vài giây]]. This saves time and fuel and reduces stress, particularly in unfamiliar cities. {{Furthermore}}, the technology has [[opened up|mở ra]] travel for people who previously felt anxious about getting lost. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, tourists can now explore the [[winding backstreets|những con hẻm quanh co]] of Tokyo or Istanbul confidently, and delivery drivers can reach dozens of addresses a day without [[prior knowledge|hiểu biết từ trước]] of the area.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, [[relying entirely on|phụ thuộc hoàn toàn vào]] apps carries certain risks. <<Giải thích>> Some research suggests that people who constantly follow digital directions develop [[weaker spatial awareness|khả năng định hướng không gian kém hơn]], since they no longer form mental maps of their surroundings. In addition, if a phone [[runs out of battery|hết pin]] or loses signal in a remote area, users may find themselves [[completely helpless|hoàn toàn bất lực]]. There have even been cases of drivers following outdated instructions onto closed roads.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> In my view, these problems are [[relatively minor|tương đối nhỏ]] and can easily be managed. Just as calculators did not make arithmetic worthless, navigation apps [[free up|giải phóng]] mental energy for other tasks while people can still learn basic map-reading skills at school or [[as a hobby|như một sở thích]]. Carrying a power bank or downloading offline maps also [[minimises the risk|giảm thiểu rủi ro]] of being stranded.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} heavy reliance on navigation apps may [[weaken our sense of direction|làm suy giảm khả năng định hướng]], the convenience, safety and freedom they provide make this trend largely positive.` },

    { id: 't2-p55', task: 2, ref: 'p55', band: '8.0', words: 277,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Global streaming platforms now allow audiences from Seoul to São Paulo to watch the same series [[on the very day|ngay trong ngày]] it is released. {{Although}} some fear that this could [[erode local cultures|làm xói mòn các nền văn hóa địa phương]], I believe the overall impact is positive.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> [[The greatest benefit|Lợi ích lớn nhất]] is that shared entertainment [[fosters cross-cultural understanding|thúc đẩy sự hiểu biết giữa các nền văn hóa]]. <<Giải thích>> When viewers watch stories set in other countries, they [[gain insight into|có được cái nhìn sâu sắc về]] different customs, values and ways of life. {{Moreover}}, streaming has given film-makers from smaller industries [[access to a worldwide audience|cơ hội tiếp cận khán giả toàn cầu]]. <<Ví dụ>> South Korean dramas and Spanish thrillers, {{for instance}}, have [[achieved international success|đạt được thành công quốc tế]] that would have been unthinkable twenty years ago, sparking interest in the languages, food and tourism of those countries.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, critics worry about [[cultural homogenisation|sự đồng nhất hóa văn hóa]]. <<Giải thích>> Because a few large companies decide which content is promoted, productions from wealthy markets, especially the United States, may [[dominate viewing habits|chi phối thói quen xem]] and [[crowd out|lấn át]] local cinema. Young people might become more familiar with American high schools than with [[their own traditions|truyền thống của chính họ]], and small domestic studios may struggle to compete with [[enormous budgets|ngân sách khổng lồ]]. Some languages and art forms could gradually [[fade into obscurity|rơi vào quên lãng]] as a result.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, I believe this risk can be managed. Several countries already require streaming services to invest a proportion of their revenue in [[locally produced content|nội dung sản xuất trong nước]], which supports national industries while still [[giving viewers choice|mang lại sự lựa chọn cho người xem]]. {{Furthermore}}, the success of non-English productions shows that global platforms do not simply [[export one culture|xuất khẩu một nền văn hóa duy nhất]]; they also allow diverse voices to [[travel in both directions|lan tỏa theo cả hai chiều]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} the dominance of a few platforms raises legitimate concerns, the ability of people worldwide to share stories is a positive development, provided that governments [[safeguard local production|bảo vệ nền sản xuất trong nước]].` },

    { id: 't2-p58', task: 2, ref: 'p58', band: '8.0', words: 284,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Enrolment in|Số lượng sinh viên đăng ký học]] humanities subjects such as history, literature and philosophy has fallen steadily in many universities. This decline is largely driven by economic pressures, and I believe it [[matters a great deal|có ý nghĩa rất lớn]] because these disciplines develop skills that society cannot [[afford to lose|có thể để mất]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reason for this trend is [[the rising cost of higher education|chi phí giáo dục đại học ngày càng tăng]]. <<Giải thích>> Students who [[take on large loans|gánh những khoản vay lớn]] understandably want degrees that lead directly to well-paid jobs, and fields such as computer science, engineering and business appear to [[offer a clearer return on investment|mang lại lợi tức đầu tư rõ ràng hơn]]. {{In addition}}, parents and governments often [[portray the humanities as|mô tả ngành khoa học xã hội và nhân văn là]] impractical, and some universities have cut funding for these departments, [[reinforcing the perception|củng cố nhận thức]] that they are less valuable. Job advertisements, too, rarely mention these degrees by name.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, this shift is [[a cause for concern|một điều đáng lo ngại]]. <<Giải thích>> Studying history helps citizens understand [[the roots of current conflicts|nguồn gốc của các xung đột hiện nay]] and avoid repeating past mistakes, {{while}} literature cultivates empathy by allowing readers to see the world through other people's eyes. Philosophy, {{meanwhile}}, trains students to [[construct logical arguments|xây dựng lập luận chặt chẽ]] and question assumptions. <<Giải thích>> These abilities are essential in [[an era of misinformation|thời đại tràn lan thông tin sai lệch]], when voters must evaluate competing claims. Even employers in the technology sector increasingly seek graduates who can [[communicate clearly|giao tiếp rõ ràng]] and [[think critically|tư duy phản biện]].

<<Thân bài 3>> <<Câu chủ đề>> To reverse this trend, universities could offer [[interdisciplinary programmes|các chương trình liên ngành]] combining humanities with technical subjects, and governments could provide scholarships for students who [[pursue these fields|theo đuổi các ngành này]]. Such reforms would show that the humanities and [[practical careers|nghề nghiệp thực tiễn]] are not [[mutually exclusive|loại trừ lẫn nhau]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, fewer students are choosing the humanities mainly for financial reasons. This matters because a society that neglects history, literature and philosophy risks producing citizens who are skilled but [[poorly equipped|thiếu trang bị]] to think deeply about ethical and political questions.` },

    { id: 't2-p59', task: 2, ref: 'p59', band: '8.0', words: 283,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Across much of the world, young people are [[abandoning|rời bỏ]] small towns and villages in search of a new life in major cities. This migration is driven mainly by [[economic and social opportunities|các cơ hội kinh tế và xã hội]], but targeted investment could make rural areas far more appealing.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> [[The primary reason|Lý do chủ yếu]] is the lack of employment in the countryside. <<Giải thích>> Agriculture, traditionally the main source of rural jobs, has become increasingly mechanised, and few modern industries [[set up operations|đặt cơ sở hoạt động]] outside urban centres. As a result, ambitious graduates feel they must move to cities to find [[well-paid positions|những vị trí được trả lương cao]] and build careers. {{Furthermore}}, cities offer better universities, hospitals and entertainment, as well as [[a more vibrant social life|một đời sống xã hội sôi động hơn]]. <<Ví dụ>> In Vietnam, {{for example}}, millions of young people from the provinces have moved to Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City, [[lured by|bị thu hút bởi]] factory jobs and higher education.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Several measures could encourage young people to stay or return. <<Giải thích>> {{Firstly}}, governments should invest in [[high-speed internet|internet tốc độ cao]], which would allow young professionals to [[work remotely|làm việc từ xa]] for city-based companies while enjoying lower living costs. {{Secondly}}, [[tax breaks|ưu đãi giảm thuế]] and grants could attract businesses to rural regions and support young entrepreneurs who wish to [[start their own ventures|khởi nghiệp]], such as eco-tourism or organic farming. {{Thirdly}}, improving local schools, clinics and transport links would make the countryside a more attractive place to [[raise a family|nuôi dạy gia đình]].

<<Thân bài 3>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Cultural life matters too. Funding for community centres, sports facilities and festivals would help rural areas [[shed their image|xóa bỏ hình ảnh]] as dull places with nothing to do.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, young people leave rural areas mainly because of limited jobs and services. By improving [[digital infrastructure|hạ tầng số]], supporting local businesses and enriching community life, governments can [[breathe new life into|thổi luồng sinh khí mới vào]] the countryside and [[slow the exodus|làm chậm làn sóng di cư]] to the cities.` },

    { id: 't2-p60', task: 2, ref: 'p60', band: '8.0', words: 277,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> {{Despite}} the vast amount of free news online, a growing number of readers are [[willing to pay for|sẵn sàng trả tiền cho]] digital subscriptions. This essay will explain why people make this choice and argue that, {{while}} [[in-depth journalism|báo chí chuyên sâu]] cannot always be free, essential news should remain [[accessible to everyone|mọi người đều có thể tiếp cận]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reason people pay is [[a desire for reliable information|mong muốn có được thông tin đáng tin cậy]]. <<Giải thích>> Free websites often depend on advertising revenue and therefore [[favour sensational headlines|ưu tiên những tiêu đề giật gân]] that attract clicks, and social media is [[flooded with|tràn ngập]] misleading stories. Subscribers believe that reputable newspapers employ professional journalists who [[verify facts|kiểm chứng sự thật]] and conduct serious investigations. {{In addition}}, paid services usually offer [[an ad-free reading experience|trải nghiệm đọc không quảng cáo]] and expert analysis. Some readers also subscribe [[as a matter of principle|như một nguyên tắc]], to support independent journalism that holds governments and corporations accountable.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, making all quality news free is [[financially unrealistic|không khả thi về mặt tài chính]]. <<Giải thích>> Investigative reporting is expensive, sometimes requiring months of research and legal advice, and without income from readers many newspapers would [[go out of business|phá sản]]. <<Ví dụ>> Hundreds of local newspapers in the United States have closed over the past two decades, leaving communities without [[reliable coverage|nguồn tin đáng tin cậy]] of local politics. Readers who pay therefore help to [[keep quality journalism alive|duy trì nền báo chí chất lượng]].

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, I believe [[information of public importance|thông tin có tầm quan trọng đối với công chúng]], such as health emergencies, natural disasters and elections, should be free for everyone. A democracy functions properly only when all citizens, [[regardless of income|bất kể thu nhập]], can make informed decisions. [[Public broadcasters|Các đài truyền hình công]] and publications offering discounted subscriptions for students and [[low-income households|các hộ gia đình thu nhập thấp]] could help to [[bridge this gap|thu hẹp khoảng cách này]].

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, people pay for news because they value accuracy, depth and independence. {{Although}} high-quality journalism needs funding, vital public information should remain free so that no one is [[left uninformed|bị bỏ lại trong tình trạng thiếu thông tin]].` },

    /* ===== Lô 6 (tự biên soạn): bài band 8 + bài band 6 kèm nhận xét 4 tiêu chí, dàn ý, từ vựng chủ đề ===== */
    { id: 't2-p61', task: 2, ref: 'p61', band: '8.0', words: 258,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Diet-related illnesses|Các bệnh liên quan đến chế độ ăn]] such as diabetes and tooth decay have become a major burden on health systems, and sugary drinks are often blamed. It has therefore been suggested that these products should be [[heavily taxed|đánh thuế nặng]]. I largely agree with this proposal, although a tax alone will not solve the problem.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The strongest argument for a tax is that [[price has a direct effect on consumption|giá cả tác động trực tiếp đến mức tiêu thụ]]. <<Giải thích>> Soft drinks are cheap, everyday purchases, so even a modest increase in price makes many people think twice, especially young consumers with limited budgets. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, after the United Kingdom introduced its levy in 2018, many manufacturers [[reformulated their products|thay đổi công thức sản phẩm]] to contain less sugar in order to avoid paying it. {{In addition}}, the revenue can be [[reinvested in public health|tái đầu tư vào y tế công cộng]], for instance by funding school sports or free drinking-water fountains.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Nevertheless}}, critics are right that such taxes can be [[regressive|lũy thoái (đánh nặng hơn lên người thu nhập thấp)]]. <<Giải thích>> Poorer households spend a larger share of their income on food and drink, so they pay proportionally more. {{Moreover}}, a tax on drinks does nothing about sugar in cakes, sweets or processed meals, and determined consumers may simply switch to these products.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{For these reasons}}, I believe the tax should be [[part of a wider strategy|một phần của chiến lược rộng hơn]]. Clear labelling, limits on advertising to children and better nutrition education would help people make [[informed choices|những lựa chọn có hiểu biết]], {{while}} subsidies for fruit and vegetables could [[offset the cost|bù đắp chi phí]] for low-income families.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, a heavy tax on sugary drinks is a sensible and effective measure, {{provided that}} it is combined with education and support for those on low incomes.`,
      band6: { text: `Nowadays many people drink a lot of sugary drinks and this is very bad for their health. Some people think the government should put a high tax on these drinks. I agree with this idea because it can help people to be more healthy.

Firstly, if the drinks are more expensive, people will buy less of them. For example, a student who has not much money will choose water instead of cola. This is good for their teeth and their weight. Moreover, many teenagers drink cola every day at school and they do not think about how much sugar is inside. If the price is higher, maybe the school shop will sell less of these drinks. Also the government can get a lot of money from the tax and they can use this money for hospitals.

Secondly, sugary drinks are very dangerous for children. Children like sweet things very much and they don't know that it is bad for them. Parents should also control what their children drink. So the tax can protect children health.

However, some people say that the tax is not fair for poor people because they have to pay more money. I think this is true but health is more important than money. If people are healthy they don't need to go to hospital and they save money. The government can also use the money from the tax to help poor families.

In conclusion, I strongly agree that the government should put a high tax on sugary drinks because it make people healthier and the government can get money for hospitals.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Có quan điểm rõ và đủ ý, nhưng phát triển chưa sâu: đoạn 2 chuyển sang trách nhiệm của phụ huynh ("Parents should also control…") – lạc khỏi câu hỏi về thuế. Phản biện về người nghèo bị gạt đi bằng "health is more important than money" mà không đưa giải pháp → TR khoảng 6.',
          CC: 'Bố cục rõ nhưng từ nối máy móc (Firstly, Secondly, Also, However, So); mỗi đoạn thiếu câu chủ đề rõ. Đoạn 2 trộn hai ý (trẻ em thích đồ ngọt + vai trò phụ huynh) → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng đơn giản và lặp: "sugary drinks", "money", "healthy" lặp nhiều lần; "a lot of", "very bad", "very dangerous" là từ chung chung. Thiếu từ chủ đề như consumption, revenue, obesity → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Có câu phức nhưng còn lỗi: "be more healthy" (→ healthier), "has not much money" (→ does not have much money), "children health" (→ children\'s health), "it make people" (→ it makes) → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: bối cảnh bệnh do chế độ ăn + nêu quan điểm (đồng ý phần lớn, nhưng thuế chưa đủ)', 'Thân 1: giá ảnh hưởng tiêu thụ + ví dụ thuế đường ở Anh (nhà sản xuất giảm đường) + tái đầu tư tiền thuế', 'Thân 2: nhượng bộ – thuế lũy thoái với người nghèo; đường còn trong đồ ăn khác', 'Quan điểm: thuế là một phần chiến lược rộng hơn (nhãn, quảng cáo, giáo dục, trợ giá rau quả)', 'Kết bài: nhắc lại quan điểm có điều kiện'],
      vocab: [['sugar levy', 'thuế đường'], ['tooth decay', 'sâu răng'], ['reformulate a product', 'thay đổi công thức sản phẩm'], ['regressive tax', 'thuế lũy thoái'], ['revenue', 'nguồn thu'], ['processed food', 'thực phẩm chế biến sẵn'], ['nutrition education', 'giáo dục dinh dưỡng'], ['subsidise', 'trợ giá'], ['informed choice', 'lựa chọn có hiểu biết']] },

    { id: 't2-p62', task: 2, ref: 'p62', band: '8.0', words: 256,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> As smartphones become [[an ever-present part of childhood|một phần luôn hiện diện trong tuổi thơ]], some people argue that children under fourteen should not be allowed to own one. While I understand the concerns behind this view, I disagree with [[an outright ban|một lệnh cấm tuyệt đối]] and believe that supervised use is more realistic.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are [[legitimate reasons|những lý do chính đáng]] to delay smartphone ownership. <<Giải thích>> Many apps are [[deliberately designed to be addictive|được thiết kế có chủ ý để gây nghiện]], and young children have [[limited self-control|khả năng tự chủ hạn chế]]. Heavy use has been linked to poor sleep, shorter attention spans and exposure to bullying or harmful content. {{It is therefore understandable that}} some parents and schools would prefer to keep phones out of children's lives altogether.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, a legal age limit would be [[almost impossible to enforce|gần như không thể thực thi]] and would ignore the genuine benefits of these devices. <<Giải thích>> Phones allow children to contact their parents when travelling alone and give them access to educational tools, from language apps to online libraries. <<Ví dụ>> {{For instance}}, a twelve-year-old who takes the bus to school is safer if she can call home when a journey is delayed. {{Furthermore}}, children who never use technology until fourteen may be [[less prepared to navigate the online world|ít được chuẩn bị để xoay xở trong thế giới mạng]] responsibly when they finally do.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> A more effective approach is [[gradual, supervised access|cho tiếp cận dần dần, có giám sát]]. Parents can use controls that [[restrict screen time|giới hạn thời gian dùng màn hình]] and block certain apps, {{while}} schools can ban phones during lessons and teach pupils about online safety.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} smartphones pose real risks to young children, banning them until fourteen is neither practical nor desirable. Guidance and limits are a better way to help children [[develop healthy digital habits|hình thành thói quen kỹ thuật số lành mạnh]].`,
      band6: { text: `In today's world, almost every child want to have a smartphone. Some people believe that children who are younger than 14 should not have a smartphone. In my opinion, I partly agree with this statement.

On the one hand, smartphones have many bad effects for children. Children use their phones for many hours every day so they don't have time for studying and doing exercise. They also can see bad things on the internet like violent videos. Some children even play games on their phone during the class, so they cannot focus on the lesson and their marks become worse. In addition, using phones at night make children sleep badly and they feel tired at school.

On the other hand, smartphones are also useful for children. They can use them to call their parents when there is a problem. Moreover, they can learn many things on the internet, for example English apps. My younger brother uses an app to practise English every day and his English is better now. Nowadays technology is very important so children need to know how to use it.

In my opinion, children should have smartphones but parents must control them. Parents can check what their children do on the phone and they can limit the time. Schools can also teach children how to use phones safely. In this way, children can enjoy the good things of smartphones and avoid the bad things.

To sum up, smartphones have both advantages and disadvantages for children. I think children under 14 can have a smartphone if their parents control it carefully.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Quan điểm không nhất quán: mở bài "partly agree" nhưng kết bài lại nói trẻ dưới 14 "can have a smartphone" – thực chất là không đồng ý. Bài viết như dạng ưu/nhược điểm thay vì trả lời "To what extent" → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Có đoạn rõ ràng và từ nối (On the one hand, In addition, Moreover), nhưng dùng "In my opinion" hai lần; đoạn quan điểm lặp lại ý thân bài 2 thay vì phát triển thêm → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng chung chung: "bad effects", "bad things", "many things", "useful"; thiếu cụm chính xác như addictive, screen time, online safety ở mức tự nhiên → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Câu đa phần đơn giản; lỗi hòa hợp chủ – vị: "every child want" (→ wants), "using phones at night make" (→ makes); "bad effects for" (→ on) → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: bối cảnh + quan điểm rõ (không đồng ý cấm tuyệt đối, ủng hộ dùng có giám sát)', 'Thân 1: nhượng bộ – lý do chính đáng để trì hoãn (gây nghiện, giấc ngủ, nội dung độc hại)', 'Thân 2: phản bác – khó thực thi, lợi ích an toàn & học tập + ví dụ đi xe buýt', 'Quan điểm: giải pháp tiếp cận dần dần (kiểm soát của phụ huynh, quy định ở trường)', 'Kết bài: nhắc lại quan điểm'],
      vocab: [['outright ban', 'lệnh cấm tuyệt đối'], ['addictive', 'gây nghiện'], ['attention span', 'khả năng tập trung'], ['enforce', 'thực thi'], ['parental controls', 'công cụ kiểm soát của phụ huynh'], ['screen time', 'thời gian dùng màn hình'], ['online safety', 'an toàn trên mạng'], ['harmful content', 'nội dung độc hại'], ['digital habits', 'thói quen kỹ thuật số']] },

    { id: 't2-p63', task: 2, ref: 'p63', band: '8.0', words: 282,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Climate change is [[one of the defining challenges of our time|một trong những thách thức lớn nhất của thời đại]], yet there is disagreement about who can do most to address it. Some believe that changes in individual behaviour are the key, while others argue that only governments and international agreements can [[make a real difference|tạo ra khác biệt thực sự]]. In my view, government action matters more, but it depends on public support that begins with individuals.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Those who emphasise personal responsibility point out that [[household consumption|tiêu dùng của các hộ gia đình]] drives a large share of emissions. <<Giải thích>> Decisions about diet, transport and energy use, multiplied across millions of people, have [[a significant cumulative effect|tác động cộng dồn đáng kể]]. {{Moreover}}, individuals who cut back on flying or eat less meat [[send a signal to businesses|phát tín hiệu cho doanh nghiệp]] that there is demand for greener products.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{On the other hand}}, many experts argue that individual choices are [[a drop in the ocean|như muối bỏ biển]] compared with the decisions of governments. <<Giải thích>> Only states can [[phase out coal power|loại bỏ dần điện than]], invest in public transport or set binding targets for industry. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a single law requiring all new cars to be electric by a certain date will reduce emissions far more than appeals to drivers to use their cars less. {{In addition}}, climate change is [[a global problem|một vấn đề toàn cầu]], so agreements such as the Paris Accord are needed to ensure that countries act together.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> I believe that the second view is more convincing, {{but}} the two approaches [[reinforce each other|củng cố lẫn nhau]]. Governments in democracies rarely introduce unpopular policies, such as carbon taxes, unless voters are willing to accept them, and that willingness grows when people already care about their own environmental impact.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} individuals can contribute through their daily choices, [[large-scale government action|hành động quy mô lớn của chính phủ]] and international cooperation are essential to tackle climate change effectively.`,
      band6: { text: `Climate change is a very big problem in the world today. Some people think that each person should change their lifestyle to solve it, but other people think the government and international agreements are more important. This essay will discuss both views and give my opinion.

Firstly, people can do many things to protect the environment. For example, they can go to work by bicycle or bus instead of car, they can turn off the lights and they can recycle their rubbish. If everyone do these things, the pollution will reduce a lot. Also people can buy products which are good for the environment. In my city, many young people now use reusable bags and bottles, and this is a good change.

Secondly, the government has more power than one person. The government can make laws to stop factories from polluting the air and they can build more public transport. Countries also need to work together because pollution go to other countries. For example, the Paris Agreement is a good example of countries working together. Moreover, the government can give money to companies which produce clean energy like solar power, so the price of clean energy will be cheaper for people.

In my opinion, I think the government is more important than individual people. One person cannot change the world but the government can make big changes. However, people should also try to help the environment in their daily life. For example, if people do not care about the environment, they will not vote for a government which wants to protect it.

In conclusion, both individuals and governments can help to solve climate change, but I believe government action is more important.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Trả lời đủ hai quan điểm và có ý kiến riêng, nhưng lập luận cho quan điểm của mình chỉ có một câu ("One person cannot change the world…") – chưa giải thích vì sao hai cách tiếp cận liên quan nhau → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Bố cục rõ, nhưng "Firstly/Secondly" dùng cho hai quan điểm đối lập là không phù hợp (nên dùng On the one hand/On the other hand); câu ví dụ lặp từ: "For example, the Paris Agreement is a good example" → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng cơ bản ("very big problem", "many things", "big changes"); thiếu từ chủ đề như emissions, renewable energy, binding targets → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi hòa hợp chủ – vị: "everyone do" (→ does), "pollution go" (→ goes); "the pollution will reduce" (→ pollution will be reduced/fall); "In my opinion, I think" thừa → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: diễn đạt lại hai quan điểm + nêu ý kiến (chính phủ quan trọng hơn nhưng cần ủng hộ từ cá nhân)', 'Thân 1: lý lẽ cho hành động cá nhân (tiêu dùng hộ gia đình, tác động cộng dồn, tín hiệu cho doanh nghiệp)', 'Thân 2: lý lẽ cho chính phủ (loại bỏ điện than, luật xe điện, hiệp định Paris)', 'Quan điểm: hai cách củng cố nhau – chính sách cần sự ủng hộ của cử tri', 'Kết bài: tóm tắt quan điểm'],
      vocab: [['carbon emissions', 'khí thải carbon'], ['cumulative effect', 'tác động cộng dồn'], ['phase out', 'loại bỏ dần'], ['binding target', 'mục tiêu có tính ràng buộc'], ['carbon tax', 'thuế carbon'], ['renewable energy', 'năng lượng tái tạo'], ['international cooperation', 'hợp tác quốc tế'], ['a drop in the ocean', 'muối bỏ biển'], ['reinforce each other', 'củng cố lẫn nhau']] },

    { id: 't2-p64', task: 2, ref: 'p64', band: '8.0', words: 265,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> When choosing new employees, companies must decide how much weight to give to [[formal qualifications|bằng cấp chính thức]] and how much to personal qualities such as attitude and character. Although both matter, I believe that personality is [[the more reliable predictor|yếu tố dự đoán đáng tin cậy hơn]] of long-term success in most jobs.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are clear reasons why many employers [[prioritise qualifications|ưu tiên bằng cấp]]. <<Giải thích>> Degrees and professional certificates provide [[objective evidence|bằng chứng khách quan]] that a candidate has the knowledge required, which is essential in fields such as medicine, law or engineering, where mistakes can be dangerous. {{In addition}}, qualifications are easy to compare, which makes recruitment [[fairer and more transparent|công bằng và minh bạch hơn]] when hundreds of people apply for the same position.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, qualifications show what a person has learned, not how they will behave at work. <<Giải thích>> Qualities such as reliability, curiosity and the ability to [[cooperate with colleagues|hợp tác với đồng nghiệp]] are difficult to teach, {{whereas}} most technical skills can be developed through training. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a graduate with excellent grades who [[refuses to accept feedback|không chịu tiếp thu góp ý]] may damage team morale, while a less qualified but enthusiastic employee often improves quickly and stays loyal to the company. Employers in customer-facing roles, such as hospitality and sales, often say that they can [[teach the job but not the attitude|dạy được công việc nhưng không dạy được thái độ]].

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> In my opinion, qualifications should act as [[a minimum requirement|yêu cầu tối thiểu]], but the final decision should depend largely on personality. Employers can assess attitude through structured interviews, [[trial periods|thời gian thử việc]] and references from previous managers.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} qualifications are necessary in many professions, personal qualities such as attitude and teamwork are generally more important, because they [[determine how well someone performs|quyết định một người làm việc tốt đến đâu]] once they are in the job.`,
      band6: { text: `When companies choose new staff, some people think that qualifications are the most important thing, while others think that personality and attitude are more important. I will discuss both sides in this essay.

On the one hand, qualifications are important because they show that a person have the knowledge for the job. For example, a doctor must have a medical degree, if not he cannot treat patients. Qualifications are also easy for companies to check, so it is a fast way to choose people. In jobs like these, a mistake can be very serious, so companies need to be sure that the person has the right knowledge.

On the other hand, personality is also very important. If a person is friendly and hard-working, they can work well with other people in the company. Some people have good degree but they are lazy or they don't like to work in a team. This is a big problem for the company. Also, people with good attitude can learn new skills quickly. For example, in my part-time job, a new staff who was very friendly learned the work faster than a person with a better degree.

In my opinion, I think personality is more important than qualifications for most jobs, but for some jobs like doctors or engineers, qualifications are necessary. Companies should look at both things when they choose staff. They can use interviews and a trial period to see the personality of the candidate.

In conclusion, qualifications and personality are both important. Companies should think about both of them, but personality is more important in my opinion.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Có quan điểm nhưng chỉ nêu ở cuối bài (mở bài không có), và giải thích vì sao tính cách quan trọng hơn còn chung chung; chưa nói cách nhà tuyển dụng đánh giá tính cách → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Bố cục rõ, có On the one hand/On the other hand. Tuy vậy kết bài lặp gần nguyên đoạn quan điểm; câu "This is a big problem for the company" liên kết lỏng → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Lặp "important" rất nhiều lần (khoảng 8 lần); từ chung chung như "good degree", "big problem"; thiếu collocation như recruitment, team morale, technical skills → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi: "a person have" (→ has); câu ghép sai dấu câu "…a medical degree, if not he cannot…" (nên tách câu hoặc dùng otherwise); thiếu mạo từ "good degree" (→ good degrees/a good degree), "good attitude" (→ a good attitude) → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: diễn đạt lại đề + nêu quan điểm (tính cách là yếu tố dự đoán tốt hơn)', 'Thân 1: lý do ưu tiên bằng cấp (bằng chứng khách quan, ngành rủi ro cao, tuyển dụng minh bạch)', 'Thân 2: lý do tính cách quan trọng (khó dạy, kỹ năng kỹ thuật đào tạo được) + ví dụ hai nhân viên', 'Quan điểm: bằng cấp là ngưỡng tối thiểu; đánh giá thái độ qua phỏng vấn, thử việc, thư giới thiệu', 'Kết bài'],
      vocab: [['formal qualifications', 'bằng cấp chính thức'], ['recruitment', 'tuyển dụng'], ['candidate', 'ứng viên'], ['predictor of success', 'yếu tố dự đoán thành công'], ['team morale', 'tinh thần làm việc của nhóm'], ['technical skills', 'kỹ năng chuyên môn'], ['structured interview', 'phỏng vấn có cấu trúc'], ['trial period', 'thời gian thử việc'], ['work ethic', 'đạo đức làm việc'], ['transparent', 'minh bạch']] },

    { id: 't2-p65', task: 2, ref: 'p65', band: '8.0', words: 258,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In many cities, [[shared electric scooters|xe scooter điện dùng chung]] that can be rented through a mobile app have appeared on the streets in large numbers. Although they offer a convenient way to travel short distances, I believe their disadvantages currently [[outweigh the benefits|lớn hơn lợi ích]] unless they are better regulated.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main advantage of e-scooters is that they provide [[a quick and flexible option|một lựa chọn nhanh và linh hoạt]] for journeys that are too long to walk but too short to drive. <<Giải thích>> They can help to solve [[the "last mile" problem|vấn đề "chặng cuối"]], allowing commuters to travel from a train station to their office without waiting for a bus. {{Furthermore}}, since they run on electricity, they produce [[no exhaust emissions|không phát thải khí xả]], so replacing even a small number of car trips could improve air quality.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, the problems they cause are serious. <<Giải thích>> Riders often travel on pavements at high speed, which puts pedestrians, especially elderly and visually impaired people, [[at risk of injury|có nguy cơ bị thương]]. Scooters are also frequently left [[blocking footpaths and entrances|chắn lối đi bộ và lối vào]]. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, Paris voted to ban rental scooters in 2023 after a rise in accidents and complaints from residents. {{In addition}}, studies suggest that many scooter journeys replace walking or cycling rather than car trips, which [[reduces their environmental benefit|làm giảm lợi ích môi trường]].

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{For these reasons}}, I believe that the drawbacks are greater at present. {{Nevertheless}}, they could be reduced through strict speed limits, [[designated parking areas|khu vực đỗ xe quy định]] and fines for riding on pavements.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{while}} rental e-scooters offer convenience and lower emissions, their impact on safety and public space means that their disadvantages currently outweigh their advantages, {{unless}} cities introduce firm rules.`,
      band6: { text: `Nowadays, electric scooters are becoming more and more popular in big cities. People can rent them easily by using an app on their phone. This essay will discuss the advantages and disadvantages of this trend.

There are many advantages of electric scooters. Firstly, they are very convenient because people can use them anytime and anywhere. For example, if you are late for work you can take a scooter and arrive quickly. Secondly, they are good for the environment because they don't use petrol. This can reduce air pollution in the city. Also, they are cheaper than a taxi, so students and young workers like to use them.

However, there are also some disadvantages. The first disadvantage is that electric scooters are dangerous. Many people ride them very fast on the pavement and they can hit people who are walking. Many accidents has happened because of this. Another disadvantage is that people leave the scooters everywhere on the street, it makes the city look messy and people cannot walk easily. In some cities, the government has already banned them because there were too many accidents and complaints from people.

In my opinion, I think the disadvantages are more than the advantages because safety is the most important thing. The government should make rules for people who ride scooters. For example, they can limit the speed of scooters and make special places to park them. People who ride on the pavement should pay a fine.

In conclusion, electric scooters have both advantages and disadvantages. I believe the disadvantages are more important.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Đề hỏi "Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?" nhưng mở bài chỉ nói "discuss the advantages and disadvantages", không trả lời câu hỏi; ý kiến chỉ xuất hiện ở đoạn 4 với một lý do ngắn → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Phân đoạn hợp lý, nhưng từ nối lặp khuôn mẫu (Firstly, Secondly, The first disadvantage, Another disadvantage); kết bài chỉ lặp lại → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng đủ dùng nhưng đơn giản: "very convenient", "dangerous", "messy"; "the disadvantages are more than the advantages" nên là outweigh; "anytime and anywhere" phóng đại → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi: "Many accidents has happened" (→ have happened); câu nối bằng dấu phẩy "…on the street, it makes the city…" (→ which makes); "In my opinion, I think" thừa → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: bối cảnh + trả lời thẳng (nhược điểm lớn hơn nếu không quản lý tốt)', 'Thân 1: lợi ích – nhanh, linh hoạt, giải quyết "chặng cuối", không phát thải', 'Thân 2: tác hại – an toàn người đi bộ, chắn lối đi, ví dụ Paris cấm 2023, thay thế đi bộ chứ không phải ô tô', 'Quan điểm: nhược điểm lớn hơn hiện tại; giảm bằng giới hạn tốc độ, khu đỗ riêng, phạt', 'Kết bài'],
      vocab: [['shared mobility', 'phương tiện dùng chung'], ['last-mile journey', 'chặng đi cuối cùng'], ['exhaust emissions', 'khí xả'], ['pavement', 'vỉa hè'], ['pedestrian', 'người đi bộ'], ['visually impaired', 'khiếm thị'], ['designated parking area', 'khu đỗ xe quy định'], ['speed limit', 'giới hạn tốc độ'], ['outweigh', 'lớn hơn, nặng hơn'], ['regulate', 'quản lý bằng quy định']] },

    { id: 't2-p66', task: 2, ref: 'p66', band: '8.0', words: 256,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> [[Volunteer tourism|Du lịch tình nguyện]], in which travellers spend their holidays teaching, building or helping with conservation projects, has become increasingly popular. Although it can benefit both visitors and local communities, I believe its disadvantages often outweigh its advantages when projects are [[poorly organised|được tổ chức kém]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Admittedly}}, volunteer holidays can be [[a rewarding experience|một trải nghiệm đáng giá]]. <<Giải thích>> Participants gain a deeper understanding of another culture than ordinary tourists, and many develop skills such as teamwork and problem-solving that are valued by employers. Well-run conservation schemes can also [[provide much-needed labour|cung cấp nguồn nhân lực rất cần thiết]], for example by monitoring endangered turtles or replanting forests that local organisations could not afford to restore on their own.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{However}}, in many cases the benefits for host communities are [[questionable|đáng ngờ]]. <<Giải thích>> Short-term volunteers usually lack the training to teach or build effectively, and they may [[take work away from local people|lấy mất việc làm của người dân địa phương]] who could be paid to do the same job. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, children in some orphanages meet a different group of foreign volunteers every few weeks, and child-protection experts warn that these [[repeated attachments and separations|những lần gắn bó rồi chia xa lặp đi lặp lại]] can harm their emotional development. {{Moreover}}, some companies charge high fees while passing only a small share to the projects themselves.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{For these reasons}}, I think volunteer tourism does more harm than good unless it is carefully regulated. Travellers should choose projects that [[match their skills|phù hợp với kỹ năng của họ]], last long enough to be useful and are led by local organisations.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, {{although}} volunteering abroad can broaden travellers' horizons, its drawbacks for vulnerable communities are significant, so the advantages only outweigh the disadvantages when projects are [[transparent and genuinely needs-based|minh bạch và thực sự xuất phát từ nhu cầu]].`,
      band6: { text: `Today, many people like to do volunteer work during their holidays, for example teaching English to children or helping to protect animals. Some people think this is a good way to travel, but others disagree. I think this trend has more advantages than disadvantages.

The first advantage is that volunteers can learn about a new culture. They live with local people and eat local food, so they understand the country better than normal tourists. They can also improve their skills like communication and teamwork, which is useful for their future job. For example, my friend went to Cambodia to teach English for a month and she said it was the best experience of her life. Furthermore, the local people get help for free.

However, there are some disadvantages. Volunteers usually only stay for one or two weeks, so they cannot help very much. Also, some volunteers don't have experience, for example they are not real teachers but they teach children. Some companies also take a lot of money from the volunteers. Sometimes the projects are not really useful, for example volunteers build a school but there are no teachers to work there.

In my opinion, the advantages is bigger than the disadvantages because both the volunteers and the local people get benefits. If volunteers choose a good company, the problems will be less. Volunteers should also stay longer and do the work that they are trained for.

In conclusion, volunteer holidays have both advantages and disadvantages, but I believe the advantages outweigh the disadvantages.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Có quan điểm từ mở bài, nhưng lý do chính "the local people get help for free" mâu thuẫn với chính đoạn 3 (tình nguyện viên ngắn hạn, thiếu kinh nghiệm). Chưa xét tác hại sâu hơn như lấy việc của người địa phương hay ảnh hưởng tới trẻ em → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Có cấu trúc đoạn rõ, nhưng đoạn 3 liệt kê ý rời rạc, mỗi ý một câu không phát triển; "Also… Some companies also…" liên kết lặp → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng phổ thông ("normal tourists", "a lot of money", "good company"); thiếu từ chủ đề như host community, conservation project, exploit → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi hòa hợp: "the advantages is bigger" (→ are greater); "the problems will be less" (→ there will be fewer problems); câu ví dụ nối dấu phẩy "…don\'t have experience, for example they are not…" → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: định nghĩa du lịch tình nguyện + quan điểm (nhược điểm thường lớn hơn nếu tổ chức kém)', 'Thân 1: nhượng bộ – lợi ích cho người đi (văn hóa, kỹ năng) và dự án bảo tồn tốt', 'Thân 2: tác hại cho cộng đồng – thiếu kỹ năng, lấy việc của dân địa phương, ví dụ trại trẻ mồ côi, công ty thu phí cao', 'Quan điểm: chỉ có lợi khi được quản lý – chọn dự án phù hợp kỹ năng, dài hạn, do tổ chức địa phương dẫn dắt', 'Kết bài'],
      vocab: [['volunteer tourism (voluntourism)', 'du lịch tình nguyện'], ['host community', 'cộng đồng tiếp nhận'], ['conservation project', 'dự án bảo tồn'], ['endangered species', 'loài có nguy cơ tuyệt chủng'], ['broaden one\'s horizons', 'mở mang tầm nhìn'], ['vulnerable', 'dễ bị tổn thương'], ['needs-based', 'xuất phát từ nhu cầu thực tế'], ['exploit', 'lợi dụng, bóc lột'], ['short-term', 'ngắn hạn']] },

    { id: 't2-p67', task: 2, ref: 'p67', band: '8.0', words: 279,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In many large cities, housing has become so expensive that young people and [[key workers|lao động thiết yếu]] such as nurses and teachers can no longer afford to live near their workplaces. This essay will examine the main causes of this problem and suggest some [[practical solutions|giải pháp thiết thực]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The most fundamental cause is that [[demand has outstripped supply|cầu vượt xa cung]]. <<Giải thích>> Jobs and opportunities are concentrated in major cities, attracting a steady flow of new residents, yet strict planning rules and high construction costs mean that relatively few new homes are built. {{In addition}}, housing is increasingly treated as [[an investment rather than a home|một khoản đầu tư hơn là nơi để ở]]. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, wealthy investors buy flats that they leave empty or rent to tourists through short-term letting platforms, which [[reduces the stock available|làm giảm nguồn nhà sẵn có]] to local people and pushes rents even higher.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> Governments can tackle the problem in several ways. <<Giải thích>> {{First}}, they should [[increase the supply of affordable housing|tăng nguồn cung nhà ở vừa túi tiền]], both by building social housing directly and by requiring private developers to include a proportion of lower-cost homes in new projects. {{Second}}, measures such as higher taxes on empty properties and limits on short-term rentals would discourage speculation. <<Ví dụ>> Cities such as Vienna, where a large share of residents live in [[subsidised rental housing|nhà cho thuê được trợ giá]], show that these policies can keep housing affordable over decades. {{Finally}}, employers and local authorities could provide [[dedicated housing for key workers|nhà ở dành riêng cho lao động thiết yếu]] close to hospitals and schools.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, the housing crisis in large cities is caused mainly by [[a shortage of homes|tình trạng thiếu nhà]] and by property speculation. A combination of new affordable housing, measures against empty and short-term properties, and targeted support for key workers would help to [[ensure that cities remain accessible|bảo đảm thành phố vẫn mở cửa]] to the people who keep them running.`,
      band6: { text: `Nowadays, houses in big cities are very expensive and many young people and workers like nurses and teachers cannot buy or rent a house. There are several reasons for this problem and there are also some solutions.

The main reason is that too many people want to live in big cities. Big cities have more jobs and better salary, so people from the countryside move there. Because of this, there are not enough houses for everyone. For example, in my city a small apartment in the centre costs more than ten years of salary of a teacher. Another reason is that rich people buy many houses to make money. They don't live in these houses, they rent them to other people with a high price.

There are some solutions for this problem. Firstly, the government should build more cheap houses for young people and workers. This will help them to have a place to live. This is important because these workers are necessary for the city, and if they live too far away, they will be tired and some of them will quit their job. Secondly, the government should make a law to stop rich people buying too many houses. Thirdly, companies can give money to their workers to help them pay the rent.

In conclusion, the price of houses in big cities is high because there are too many people and rich people buy many houses. The government and companies should do something to solve this problem, for example build cheap houses and make laws.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Trả lời đủ hai phần (nguyên nhân, giải pháp) nhưng giải pháp chỉ liệt kê, không giải thích vì sao hiệu quả hay đưa ví dụ thực tế; "make a law to stop rich people buying too many houses" mơ hồ, khó thực hiện → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Bố cục hợp lý nhưng đoạn giải pháp chỉ là danh sách Firstly/Secondly/Thirdly, mỗi ý một câu; kết bài lặp lại gần như toàn bộ thân bài → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Lặp "houses", "big cities", "rich people", "cheap houses"; thiếu từ chủ đề như affordable housing, supply, speculation, social housing; "better salary" (→ higher salaries) → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Chủ yếu câu đơn và ghép; lỗi: câu nối bằng dấu phẩy "They don\'t live in these houses, they rent them…"; "with a high price" (→ at a high price); "stop rich people buying" chấp nhận được nhưng thiếu đa dạng cấu trúc → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: nêu vấn đề (nhà đắt, lao động thiết yếu bị đẩy ra xa) + giới thiệu nguyên nhân & giải pháp', 'Thân 1: nguyên nhân – cầu vượt cung (việc làm tập trung, quy hoạch chặt), nhà thành công cụ đầu tư, cho thuê ngắn hạn', 'Thân 2: giải pháp – nhà ở xã hội & tỉ lệ nhà giá rẻ bắt buộc, thuế nhà bỏ trống, hạn chế cho thuê ngắn hạn, ví dụ Vienna, nhà cho lao động thiết yếu', 'Kết bài: tóm tắt nguyên nhân và giải pháp'],
      vocab: [['affordable housing', 'nhà ở vừa túi tiền'], ['key workers', 'lao động thiết yếu'], ['social housing', 'nhà ở xã hội'], ['property speculation', 'đầu cơ bất động sản'], ['short-term letting', 'cho thuê ngắn hạn'], ['planning restrictions', 'quy định hạn chế về quy hoạch'], ['outstrip', 'vượt quá'], ['housing stock', 'quỹ nhà ở'], ['rent control', 'kiểm soát giá thuê nhà'], ['commute', 'đi lại giữa nhà và nơi làm việc']] },

    { id: 't2-p68', task: 2, ref: 'p68', band: '8.0', words: 274,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Linguists estimate that [[a significant proportion|một tỉ lệ đáng kể]] of the world's roughly seven thousand languages could disappear by the end of this century. This essay will explain why so many languages are [[under threat|đang bị đe dọa]] and suggest how they can be protected.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> The main reason for this decline is that minority languages offer fewer [[economic and social advantages|lợi thế kinh tế và xã hội]] than dominant ones. <<Giải thích>> Parents who want their children to succeed often encourage them to speak the national language or English, which are needed for education, jobs and the internet. Over time, children grow up understanding their grandparents' language but no longer speaking it, and the chain of [[intergenerational transmission|sự truyền lại giữa các thế hệ]] is broken. {{In addition}}, urbanisation brings people from different communities together in cities, where [[a common language|một ngôn ngữ chung]] is needed for daily life. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, many indigenous languages in Australia now have only a few elderly speakers.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Fortunately}}, there are several ways to [[reverse this trend|đảo ngược xu hướng này]]. <<Giải thích>> {{First}}, governments can give minority languages official status and fund schools where children are taught in them, at least in the early years. <<Ví dụ>> The revival of Welsh, which is now used in schools, television and government services, shows that [[such support can work|sự hỗ trợ như vậy có thể hiệu quả]]. {{Second}}, technology can help: recording elderly speakers, creating online dictionaries and adding languages to translation apps makes them visible and useful for young people. {{Finally}}, communities themselves play [[a crucial role|vai trò then chốt]] by using the language at home and in local events, so that children see it as part of modern life rather than the past.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, languages are disappearing mainly because dominant languages offer greater opportunities. However, official recognition, education, technology and [[community pride|niềm tự hào cộng đồng]] can help to keep endangered languages alive.`,
      band6: { text: `Today many languages in the world are disappearing. This is a serious problem because language is an important part of culture. If a language disappears, the stories, songs and knowledge of that community will also disappear. In this essay I will explain the reasons and give some solutions.

The first reason is globalization. Nowadays English is very popular and people need English to find a good job. So many young people only learn English and they forget their own language. The second reason is that young people move to the cities. In the city everyone speak the main language of the country, so they don't use their small language anymore. Moreover, on the internet and TV most programs are in English or the main language, so children prefer these languages.

There are some ways to protect the languages. The government should teach these languages in schools, so children can learn them. Also they can make TV programs and websites in these languages. Technology can also help, for example people can record the old people speaking and put it on the internet, so the next generation can still learn. Old people should teach their language to the young people in the family. For example, in my country some families still speak their local language at home.

In conclusion, many languages are disappearing because of globalization and young people moving to the city. The government and families should work together to save these languages. If we do nothing, many languages will be lost forever.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Trả lời đủ hai phần nhưng ý còn nông: "people only learn English and they forget their own language" khái quát quá mức; ví dụ "in my country some families…" mơ hồ, không minh họa được giải pháp có hiệu quả → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Phân đoạn rõ (The first reason, The second reason), nhưng đoạn giải pháp liệt kê, không có câu chủ đề hay liên kết giữa các ý; "Also they can…" không rõ "they" chỉ ai → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Dùng được "globalization" nhưng nhiều từ không chính xác: "small language" (→ minority language), "old people" (→ elderly speakers); thiếu từ như endangered, indigenous, revival → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi: "everyone speak" (→ speaks); câu bắt đầu bằng "So" trong văn trang trọng; câu đa phần đơn giản, ít mệnh đề quan hệ hay câu phức → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: số liệu ngôn ngữ có nguy cơ biến mất + giới thiệu hai phần', 'Thân 1: nguyên nhân – ngôn ngữ chủ đạo mang lại cơ hội, đứt gãy truyền lại giữa các thế hệ, đô thị hóa; ví dụ ngôn ngữ bản địa Úc', 'Thân 2: giải pháp – vị thế chính thức & trường học (ví dụ tiếng Wales), công nghệ (ghi âm, từ điển, app dịch), vai trò cộng đồng', 'Kết bài'],
      vocab: [['endangered language', 'ngôn ngữ có nguy cơ biến mất'], ['minority language', 'ngôn ngữ thiểu số'], ['indigenous', 'bản địa'], ['intergenerational transmission', 'truyền lại giữa các thế hệ'], ['dominant language', 'ngôn ngữ chủ đạo'], ['official status', 'vị thế chính thức'], ['language revival', 'sự hồi sinh ngôn ngữ'], ['cultural heritage', 'di sản văn hóa'], ['urbanisation', 'đô thị hóa']] },

    { id: 't2-p69', task: 2, ref: 'p69', band: '8.0', words: 267,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> Video games, once seen as a hobby for children, are now played for several hours a day by millions of people of all ages. In my view, their popularity comes from their ability to [[combine challenge, achievement and social contact|kết hợp thử thách, thành tựu và giao tiếp xã hội]], and this is a largely positive development, provided that playing is kept [[in moderation|ở mức vừa phải]].

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> There are several reasons why gaming has become so popular. <<Giải thích>> Modern games are carefully designed to give players [[a constant sense of progress|cảm giác liên tục tiến bộ]], through levels, rewards and new skills to master. {{In addition}}, online games have become [[a social space|một không gian xã hội]] where friends meet, cooperate and compete, which is particularly attractive for young people who live far apart. <<Ví dụ>> {{Moreover}}, smartphones mean that games are available everywhere, so people can play on the bus or during a lunch break.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> I believe this trend is mainly positive. <<Giải thích>> Many games [[develop valuable skills|phát triển những kỹ năng có giá trị]] such as problem-solving, strategic thinking and quick decision-making, and team-based games require communication and cooperation. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, some surgeons have been found to perform better in tasks that require precise hand movements after regular gaming. Games also offer [[an affordable form of entertainment|một hình thức giải trí rẻ]] and a way to relax after work or study.

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{That said}}, [[excessive gaming|chơi game quá mức]] can cause real harm. Playing late into the night affects sleep, and some players, especially teenagers, spend money on in-game purchases or neglect their studies. Clear time limits and [[parental guidance|sự hướng dẫn của phụ huynh]] are therefore important.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, video games are popular because they are rewarding, social and easy to access. As long as people play in moderation, I consider this to be [[a positive rather than a negative development|một xu hướng tích cực hơn là tiêu cực]].`,
      band6: { text: `Nowadays, many people spend a lot of time playing video games every day. Some people think it is a waste of time, but others enjoy it very much. There are some reasons for this and I think it is both positive and negative.

There are many reasons why people like video games. Firstly, video games are very fun and exciting. People can forget about their stress when they play. Secondly, people can play with their friends online, so they don't feel lonely. Thirdly, it is very easy to play games because everyone has a smartphone now. Fourthly, many games are free, so people do not need to pay money to start playing.

In my opinion, playing video games has some good points. It helps people to relax after a long day of work or study. Some games also help people to think fast and solve problems. However, playing too much games is bad. Some people play all night and they cannot sleep enough, so they are tired the next day. Children who play too much games often have bad results at school. Also, some games are very violent and it can make children aggressive. Moreover, some people spend a lot of money to buy things inside the game, and it is a waste of money.

In conclusion, people play video games because they are fun, social and easy to play. I think it is positive if people play a little, but it is negative if they play too much. Parents should control the time that their children play.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Phần 2 của đề ("is it positive or negative?") bị trả lời nước đôi: mở bài nói "both positive and negative", kết bài "positive if people play a little" – không có lập trường rõ. Ý "violent games make children aggressive" đưa ra mà không có căn cứ → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Đoạn 2 liệt kê lý do máy móc (Firstly/Secondly/Thirdly); đoạn 3 gộp cả mặt tốt và mặt xấu nên dài và thiếu trọng tâm → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng đơn giản: "very fun and exciting", "good points", "bad results"; thiếu cụm như in moderation, sense of achievement, in-game purchases → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi: "too much games" (→ too many games / too much gaming) lặp hai lần; "some games are very violent and it can make" (→ they can make); "cannot sleep enough" (→ do not get enough sleep) → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: bối cảnh + trả lời ngắn cả hai phần (vì thử thách, thành tựu, giao tiếp; nhìn chung tích cực nếu vừa phải)', 'Thân 1: lý do phổ biến – cảm giác tiến bộ, không gian xã hội, điện thoại thông minh', 'Thân 2: vì sao tích cực – kỹ năng tư duy, hợp tác, ví dụ bác sĩ phẫu thuật, giải trí rẻ', 'Đoạn nhượng bộ: tác hại khi chơi quá mức (giấc ngủ, mua vật phẩm, bỏ bê học) + giới hạn thời gian', 'Kết bài'],
      vocab: [['in moderation', 'ở mức vừa phải'], ['sense of achievement', 'cảm giác thành tựu'], ['strategic thinking', 'tư duy chiến lược'], ['in-game purchases', 'mua vật phẩm trong game'], ['excessive gaming', 'chơi game quá mức'], ['addictive design', 'thiết kế gây nghiện'], ['multiplayer', 'nhiều người chơi'], ['leisure activity', 'hoạt động giải trí'], ['neglect one\'s studies', 'bỏ bê việc học']] },

    { id: 't2-p70', task: 2, ref: 'p70', band: '8.0', words: 296,
      text: `<<Mở bài>> In the past, many people stayed with one employer for most of their working lives, but today it is common to change jobs every few years. I believe this shift is driven by changes in both the economy and personal expectations, and that it is [[a largely positive development|một xu hướng phần lớn là tích cực]] for individuals.

<<Thân bài 1>> <<Câu chủ đề>> One major reason is that [[job security|sự ổn định công việc]] has declined. <<Giải thích>> Companies now restructure frequently in response to new technology and global competition, so employees can no longer expect a job for life and [[feel less loyalty|ít cảm thấy gắn bó]] in return. {{In addition}}, attitudes have changed: younger workers in particular value personal growth, flexibility and meaningful work, and they are willing to move when a role [[no longer meets these expectations|không còn đáp ứng những kỳ vọng này]]. <<Ví dụ>> Online recruitment platforms have also made it much easier to discover and apply for new opportunities.

<<Thân bài 2>> <<Câu chủ đề>> In my view, this trend [[brings more benefits than drawbacks|mang lại nhiều lợi ích hơn bất lợi]]. <<Giải thích>> Moving between employers allows people to gain a wider range of skills and experience, which makes them more adaptable in a fast-changing labour market. <<Ví dụ>> {{For example}}, a marketing assistant who works in both a start-up and a large corporation learns different ways of working that neither company alone could teach. Changing jobs is also often [[the quickest route to a higher salary|con đường nhanh nhất để có lương cao hơn]].

<<Quan điểm>> <<Câu chủ đề>> {{Admittedly}}, frequent moves have costs. Employers lose [[valuable expertise|chuyên môn quý giá]] and must spend money on recruitment and training, while individuals may give up pension benefits or find it hard to build deep relationships at work. {{However}}, these disadvantages can be reduced if companies invest in their staff and offer genuine career paths.

<<Kết bài>> {{In conclusion}}, people change jobs more often because of reduced job security and new expectations about work. {{Although}} this creates some challenges, I believe it is mainly positive because it helps workers to [[develop and adapt throughout their careers|phát triển và thích nghi trong suốt sự nghiệp]].`,
      band6: { text: `In the past, people usually worked for one company for their whole life. However, nowadays people often change their job. In this essay I will explain why this happens and whether it is a positive or negative development.

There are two main reasons why people change jobs more often today. The first reason is money. If another company offers a higher salary, people will move to that company. The second reason is that people get bored when they do the same work for a long time. They want to try new things and learn new skills. Moreover, now it is very easy to find a new job on the internet. For example, my cousin changed his job three times in five years because each new company paid him more.

I think this is a positive development. When people change jobs, they can learn many different skills and get more experience. This is good for their career. They can also earn more money and find a job which they really enjoy. Changing jobs is also exciting because people can meet new colleagues.

However, there are some negative sides. Companies have to spend money to train new staffs, and it is not good for them. Also people who change job too often may look unreliable to employers. Companies may also lose good workers who know the company very well.

In conclusion, people change jobs more often because of money and boring work. I believe it is a positive development for workers, although it has some disadvantages for companies.`,
        notes: {
          TR: 'Trả lời đủ hai phần và có quan điểm, nhưng nguyên nhân chỉ ở mức cá nhân (tiền, chán việc), bỏ qua nguyên nhân lớn như doanh nghiệp tái cấu trúc, công việc kém ổn định; các ý phát triển ngắn, không có ví dụ cụ thể → TR 6.',
          CC: 'Bố cục rõ; tuy vậy "Moreover" đặt cuối đoạn 2 nối một ý không cùng loại (internet ≠ động cơ cá nhân); kết bài viết "because of money and boring work" làm đơn giản hóa → CC 6.',
          LR: 'Từ vựng đủ dùng nhưng lặp ("money", "change jobs", "good for"); "boring work" thay vì unfulfilling work; thiếu các cụm như job security, career progression, labour market → LR 6.',
          GRA: 'Lỗi: "new staffs" (staff không đếm được → new staff); "change job too often" (→ change jobs); "it is not good for them" mơ hồ; câu phần lớn đơn giản → GRA 6.',
        } },
      outline: ['Mở bài: so sánh quá khứ – hiện tại + trả lời cả hai phần (do kinh tế & kỳ vọng; phần lớn tích cực)', 'Thân 1: nguyên nhân – công việc kém ổn định (tái cấu trúc), thay đổi giá trị của người trẻ, nền tảng tuyển dụng trực tuyến', 'Thân 2: vì sao tích cực – kỹ năng đa dạng, dễ thích nghi, ví dụ trợ lý marketing, tăng lương', 'Nhượng bộ: chi phí cho doanh nghiệp & cá nhân, cách giảm thiểu', 'Kết bài'],
      vocab: [['job security', 'sự ổn định công việc'], ['job-hopping', 'nhảy việc'], ['restructure', 'tái cấu trúc'], ['career progression', 'thăng tiến nghề nghiệp'], ['labour market', 'thị trường lao động'], ['adaptable', 'dễ thích nghi'], ['staff turnover', 'tỉ lệ nghỉ việc'], ['pension benefits', 'quyền lợi lương hưu'], ['meaningful work', 'công việc có ý nghĩa']] },
  ]);
})(typeof self !== 'undefined' ? self : this);
